The core problem this project solves
A basic RAG demo does: embed a question → find similar chunks → stuff them in a prompt → ask an LLM. That works fine on easy questions and falls apart silently on hard ones — and "silently" is the issue. CITADEL exists to make retrieval and answer quality measurable, and to combine two retrieval strategies that catch different kinds of misses.
1. Hybrid retrieval: why two search methods, not one
Vector search (semantic): your question gets converted into a vector — a list of numbers capturing its meaning — using bge-small-en-v1.5, a local embedding model. Every chunk of your corpus was embedded the same way ahead of time and stored in Qdrant. At query time, Qdrant finds the chunks whose vectors are closest (cosine similarity) to your question's vector. This catches paraphrases — if you ask "how do rockets get reused?" it can find a chunk about "reusable launch systems" even with zero shared words.
BM25 (keyword search): this is the older, statistical approach — it scores documents by how often your exact query terms appear, weighted by how rare those terms are across the whole corpus (so common words like "the" barely count, but a rare term like "Sputnik" counts a lot). It catches exact terminology that vector search sometimes blurs past — acronyms, proper nouns, specific model numbers.
Why combine them: each method has blind spots the other doesn't. Vector search can miss an exact rare term because it's reasoning in "meaning space," not lexical space. BM25 can miss a paraphrase because it only sees word overlap. Hybrid search runs both, gets the top 20 candidates from each, and merges them.
The merge method — Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF): the tricky part is that BM25 scores (raw term-frequency math, can be any positive number) and cosine similarity scores (always between -1 and 1) are on completely incompatible scales — you can't just average them. RRF sidesteps this by ignoring the actual scores and using only rank position. For each chunk, its RRF score is the sum of 1/(60+rank) across every list it appears in. A chunk that shows up at rank 2 in BM25 and rank 5 in vector search gets credit from both; a chunk that only one method found gets credit from just that one. This means chunks both methods agree on naturally rise to the top — consensus is rewarded without needing to calibrate two different scoring systems against each other. I actually unit-tested this logic early on with synthetic data to confirm a "consensus" document correctly outranked documents only one retriever liked.
2. Reranking — a second, smarter pass
Hybrid retrieval gives you 20 decent candidates, but "decent" isn't the same as "the best 5." A reranker is a model specifically trained to look at a (query, chunk) pair and score how relevant that chunk actually is to that specific question — it's slower and more expensive than initial retrieval, which is exactly why it only runs on the 20 survivors instead of your whole corpus.
CITADEL uses Cohere's Rerank API (free tier) as the primary reranker, with a local cross-encoder (ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2) as an automatic fallback if no Cohere key is set. A cross-encoder is architecturally different from the embedding model used earlier: instead of encoding the query and the chunk separately into vectors and comparing them (what the embedding model does), a cross-encoder feeds the query and chunk into the model together, so it can directly reason about how they relate to each other. That's more accurate but too slow to run against an entire corpus — which is exactly why it's a second-stage filter on 20 candidates, not the first-stage search over thousands.
Why measure this instead of assuming it helps: that's the actual point of the project. The A/B toggle in app/pipeline.py lets the same 15-question eval set run once with reranking and once without, isolating its actual measured effect on RAGAS scores rather than just asserting "reranking improves things" without evidence.
3. RAGAS — turning "the answer looks fine" into actual numbers
RAGAS is an evaluation framework built specifically for RAG systems. Instead of a human reading 30 answers and going "yeah, looks okay," it uses an LLM as an automated judge against four specific, narrow questions:

Faithfulness: does every claim in the answer actually appear in the retrieved context, or did the model add something not supported by the sources (a hallucination)? RAGAS does this by breaking the answer into individual factual statements and checking each one against the context.
Answer relevancy: does the answer actually address the question asked, or does it wander off-topic while still being technically "faithful"?
Context precision: of the chunks retrieval pulled back, how many were actually useful? High precision means low noise.
Context recall: did retrieval pull back everything needed to fully answer the question, compared against your hand-written ground-truth answer? This catches cases where the system missed a needed chunk entirely.

Why this needed a "judge" LLM: these aren't keyword-matching checks — "is this claim supported by this paragraph" is a judgment call that needs language understanding. RAGAS defaults to using OpenAI for that judge role. Since this project commits to zero paid APIs, the judge was rewired to use the same local Ollama model that generates your answers (langchain_ollama.ChatOllama, wrapped in LangchainLLMWrapper to satisfy RAGAS's expected interface).
Why unanswerable questions were deliberately included: faithfulness is most visible exactly when there's nothing to support an answer. Asking a question the corpus genuinely can't answer (like ISS wifi passwords) and checking the system says "I cannot answer this from the provided sources" instead of inventing something is the simplest, highest-signal hallucination test you can build.
4. Generation — Ollama, and why "local" was the right fix, not just a workaround
Ollama runs LLMs entirely on your own machine — no API key, no network call, no rate limit, no per-token cost. You pulled llama3.1:8b, a relatively small (for an LLM) 8-billion-parameter model that's still capable enough to do grounded question-answering and to serve as RAGAS's judge. The trade-off you felt directly: CPU inference is much slower than a hosted API — that's the real cost of "fully free, zero dependencies," and it's worth being upfront about that trade-off if asked, since it's an honest engineering decision, not a weakness.
The generation prompt itself enforces groundedness through instruction, not just hope: the system prompt explicitly tells the model to answer only from the provided excerpts, to say it can't answer rather than guess, and to cite sources by bracket number — this is what RAGAS's faithfulness metric is actually testing against.
5. Langfuse — observability, the part that gave you the most trouble
Langfuse records what happened inside a single query: which chunks got retrieved, what order reranking put them in, what prompt went to the LLM, what came back, and how long each stage took. It's not part of the answer-quality logic at all — RAGAS scores come straight from your pipeline's return values, independent of whether Langfuse is even running. It exists purely so that after the fact, you (or an interviewer) can click into any single query and see its internal trace tree instead of just trusting the final answer was reasonable.
This is also worth mentioning honestly if asked: this piece had real version-compatibility friction (the official Python SDK had moved to a newer architecture than the easy-to-self-host server version), which is itself a legitimate, relatable engineering story — dependency drift between a client library and a self-hosted service is an extremely common real-world headache, and diagnosing it methodically (checking actual installed versions and real method signatures instead of guessing) is a good thing to describe in an interview, not something to hide.
How it all fits together, end to end
Question → [BM25 + Vector search] → RRF fusion (top 20) 
         → Reranker (top 5) → Ollama generation with citations
         → Answer, every stage timed and logged to Langfuse
And separately, offline: the 15-question golden set runs through that same pipeline twice (rerank on/off), and RAGAS scores both runs so you have actual numbers proving what reranking bought you — not just an assumption that it helped.
